       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · The Verge · 2026-09-22 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Activision to Lead Halo and Sea of Thieves in Xbox Overhaul</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Microsoft</span> <span>#Activision</span> <span>#Halo</span> <span>#Gaming</span></div>
       <p>Microsoft has announced a significant restructuring of its Xbox division, marking a major shift in how its game franchises are managed. This move comes months after the company laid off 1,600 Xbox employees and sold off several studios. Under the new organizational structure, Activision will assume direct control of three prominent franchises: Halo, Sea of Thieves, and Age of Empires.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-22/activision-to-lead-halo-and-sea-of-thieves-in-xbox-overhaul.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The announcement coincides with further workforce reductions, as Microsoft is laying off approximately 268 additional Xbox employees as part of the broader shake-up. This consolidation represents a strategic pivot for the company, centralizing the management of its most iconic titles under the Activision banner. By integrating these franchises, Microsoft aims to streamline development pipelines and enhance operational efficiency across its portfolio.</p><p>The restructuring reflects a continued effort to optimize the Xbox brand following recent financial pressures and market adjustments. While specific details on the operational changes remain limited, the transfer of these flagship titles signals a new chapter for their respective development teams. The company has not provided further commentary on long-term strategic goals beyond the immediate organizational changes.</p><p>What to watch: How this consolidation impacts the development roadmaps for Halo, Sea of Thieves, and Age of Empires.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately reflects the source material from The Verge, correctly reporting Microsoft's Xbox restructuring, Activision's acquisition of Halo, Sea of Thieves, and Age of Empires, and the layoff numbers. No contradictions or fabricated claims found.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theverge.com/news/998586/activision-halo-xbox-reorganization-layoffs" rel="noopener">The Verge</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
