     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · India Today · 2026-09-22 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Bombay High Court Criticizes Vijay Mallya for Sharing ED Affidavit on X</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Vijay Mallya</span> <span>#Bombay High Court</span> <span>#Enforcement Directorate</span> <span>#social media</span></div>
       <p>The Bombay High Court has issued a strong rebuke to businessman Vijay Mallya regarding his decision to share an affidavit from the Enforcement Directorate (ED) on the social media platform X. The court explicitly stated that such an action &quot;harms your own case,&quot; signaling its disapproval of Mallya's conduct in disseminating official legal documents publicly.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-22/bombay-high-court-criticizes-vijay-mallya-for-sharing-ed-aff.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This judicial observation highlights concerns over the appropriate handling of court-related materials. The act of making an ED affidavit public on X drew direct criticism from the High Court, emphasizing the potential negative consequences for Mallya's legal standing. The court's remarks underscore the gravity with which it views the public disclosure of documents pertinent to ongoing legal proceedings.</p><p>While the specifics of the ED affidavit and the broader context of Mallya's legal battles were not detailed in the court's public statement on this particular issue, the clear message was about the impropriety of his social media activity. The court's admonition serves as a reminder of the protocols surrounding legal documentation and the potential repercussions of disregarding them. The statement &quot;It harms your own case&quot; delivers a direct warning about the impact of such actions on an individual's defense or legal strategy.</p><p>What to watch: Any further judicial observations or actions related to Mallya's social media conduct.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately reflects the source summary regarding the Bombay High Court's criticism of Vijay Mallya. The elaboration on the implications of the court's remarks is reasonable and does not introduce contradictory facts.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.indiatoday.in/india/law-news/story/bombay-high-court-fugitive-businessman-vijay-mallya-ed-affidavit-x-undermines-efficacy-of-court-3000485-2026-09-22?utm_source=rss" rel="noopener">India Today</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
