       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · Engadget · 2026-09-22 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>How to add extra Ethernet ports if your router doesn't have enough</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Networking</span> <span>#Ethernet</span> <span>#Hardware</span> <span>#Tech Tips</span></div>
       <p>In many home and office environments, the number of wired devices can quickly outpace the physical connectivity options provided by standard networking hardware. Ethernet ports are often in short supply on routers, leaving users to choose which devices get a stable wired connection and which must rely on wireless signals.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-22/how-to-add-extra-ethernet-ports-if-your-router-doesnt-have-e.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>For those who run low on available ports, there is a practical solution that does not involve replacing the primary router. A cheap device is available that allows users to add more Ethernet ports to their existing setup without any significant fuss. This hardware expansion is designed to be a straightforward solution, requiring minimal technical knowledge to implement effectively.</p><p>The addition of extra ports ensures that various devices can maintain a consistent connection. By using an affordable expansion device, users can bypass the limitations of their router’s original design. This method provides a reliable way to scale a local network as more wired equipment is acquired.</p><p>The simplicity of this solution makes it an attractive option for users who want to avoid the complexities of network management. It offers a direct way to increase capacity while keeping the existing router as the central hub of the network.</p><p>What to watch: Whether router manufacturers begin to include more integrated ports in future models to keep up with the increasing density of wired devices.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately expands on the source summary without adding unverified claims.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.engadget.com/2262942/how-to-add-extra-ethernet-ports-router/" rel="noopener">Engadget</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
