     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-09-22 · editor 6/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Rajya Sabha Polls Set for October 16 in 12 Seats</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Indian Politics</span> <span>#Rajya Sabha</span> <span>#By-elections</span> <span>#BSP</span></div>
       <p>By-elections for 12 Rajya Sabha seats are scheduled to take place on October 16. The polls are triggered by the vacancy created by the resignation of BSP MP Ramji Gautam. </p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-22/rajya-sabha-polls-set-for-october-16-in-12-seats.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This development marks a significant shift for the Bahujan Samaj Party. With Gautam vacating his seat, the party will be left without any representation in the Upper House of Parliament. This will be the first time since 1989 that the BSP has held zero seats in the Rajya Sabha. </p><p>The vacancy represents a crucial opportunity for other political forces in Uttar Pradesh. Analysts suggest that the Samajwadi Party or its allies may secure this seat. The outcome could alter the balance of power in the state’s legislative representation at the national level. </p><p>Simultaneously, another significant by-election is underway in West Bengal. A seat has been vacated following the resignation of a Trinamool Congress MP. The BJP is widely expected to gain this seat, marking a strategic expansion for the national party in the state. </p><p>These by-elections are critical for the ruling coalition and opposition alliances. The results on October 16 will provide a clear indication of the shifting political dynamics in key states. </p><p>The loss of a Rajya Sabha seat for the BSP underscores the volatile nature of regional politics in Uttar Pradesh. Meanwhile, a potential BJP win in West Bengal would signal a continued presence in a state traditionally dominated by the TMC. </p><p>What to watch: The final vote count in Uttar Pradesh and West Bengal on October 16 to determine the new composition of the Rajya Sabha.</p>
       
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/rajya-sabha-polls-in-12-seats-on-october-16/article71495593.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="article/2026-09-22/rajya-sabha-polls-set-for-october-16-in-12-seats.html" data-title="Rajya Sabha Polls Set for October 16 in 12 Seats"><b>Share:</b>
