       <h1>Xbox Continues 'Reset' with Job Cuts and Activision Developing Next Halo Title</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Xbox</span> <span>#Gaming</span> <span>#Activision</span> <span>#Halo</span></div>
       <p>Xbox's ongoing &quot;reset&quot; has seen further significant developments, including 268 job cuts across its operations. This move is part of a turbulent period for the gaming giant, which has been navigating substantial changes throughout 2026.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-09-22/xbox-continues-reset-with-job-cuts-and-activision-developing.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>A notable shift in its flagship franchise strategy has also been announced: Activision is slated to develop the next installment of the iconic Halo game series. This represents a significant change in the creative direction and production of one of Xbox's most recognizable titles.</p><p>The job reductions and strategic adjustments underscore what the summary describes as a turbulent 2026 for Xbox. These internal restructuring efforts aim to refine the company's operational footprint and focus areas.</p><p>Further impacting its internal studio landscape, there is a probable closure of Ninja Theory, the studio behind titles such as Hellblade: Senua's Sacrifice. This potential closure signals a consolidation or realignment of Xbox's in-house development teams. These collective actions reflect a period of intense transformation within the Xbox ecosystem.</p><p>What to watch: The long-term impact of these structural changes on Xbox's game development pipeline and its competitive standing in the gaming industry.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article properly reports Xbox's job cuts, Activision's Halo development plans, and Ninja Theory's likely closure while using appropriate qualifiers for uncertainty.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
